// Saturn V (1969).
// S-IC, S-II and S-IVB, the adapter with the lunar module in it, then the service module and the
// command module with its docking probe. The adapter's four panels open as the CSM lets go, leaving
// the lunar module on the S-IVB to be docked with and pulled out, its legs folded up its descent stage
// until it goes to land.
// Built by hand from the generator's output. Each stage is its tank, with skirts round its domes
// and nothing around it. The S-IVB carries four APS thrusters for attitude
// control between its burns. The S-II lights from four solid ullage motors
// in the S-IC/S-II interstage and the S-IVB from two on its aft skirt, as the real ones did;
// a stage cannot start its J-2s without settled propellant (the S-IVB, with its two starts,
// settles for the second one with the service module's RCS, which carries the real CSM's
// 550 kg).
{
  id: "saturn_v",
  name: "Saturn V",
  parts: [
    {
      id: "command_module",
      name: "Command Module",
      shape: {
        compound: [
          {
            name: "skin",
            shape: {
              revolved: {
                walls: [
                  {
                    name: "aft_skirt",
                    curve: { start: [1.95, 0], segments: [{ line: [1.95, 1.085] }] },
